# Wire

A single static `index.html` you host on GitHub Pages. Visitors never create
an account — you create a username/password for each person ahead of time
and tell it to them yourself. Once logged in, anyone can post in the shared
**Everyone** room or open a private 1:1 thread with anyone else. Everything
is stored in Firestore, so history persists across sessions like a normal
messaging app.

## Why it needs Firebase at all

GitHub Pages only serves static files — it can't hold a live database or
push real-time updates. Firebase's free **Spark** plan supplies that part
(login + real-time storage) with no credit card required, while GitHub
Pages keeps serving the page itself. Confirmed current Spark free quotas:
50,000 monthly active Auth users, 1 GiB Firestore storage, 50,000 reads /
20,000 writes / 20,000 deletes per day, 10 GiB egress a month — far more
than a small private chat needs. Two things on Firebase always require the
paid Blaze plan regardless of volume: Cloud Storage and Cloud Functions.
This project uses neither, so it stays fully free.

## One-time setup

### 1. Create the Firebase project
1. Go to [console.firebase.google.com](https://console.firebase.google.com) → **Add project** → give it a name → you can decline Google Analytics.
2. **Build → Authentication → Get started → Sign-in method → Email/Password → Enable.**
3. **Build → Firestore Database → Create database** → start in **production mode** → pick any region.
4. **Project settings (gear icon) → General → Your apps → Web (`</>`)** → register an app (no need for Firebase Hosting) → copy the `firebaseConfig` object it shows you.

### 2. Wire up `index.html`
Open `index.html` and paste your copied values into the `firebaseConfig`
object near the top of the `<script type="module">` block. These values
are not secret — Firestore/Auth rules are what actually restrict access,
not hiding this object.

### 3. Publish the security rules
Firestore Database → **Rules** tab → paste in the contents of
`firestore.rules` from this repo → **Publish**. Without this step your
data defaults to locked-down (nobody can read/write) or, if you chose test
mode, wide open — either way, publish the rules here.

### 4. Create your first accounts
The admin script creates accounts; the website itself never lets anyone
sign up.
1. Project settings → **Service accounts** → **Generate new private key** → save the download as `serviceAccountKey.json` in this folder. Keep it private — it's already in `.gitignore` so it won't get pushed to GitHub.
2. `npm install`
3. `node create-user.js add alice "some strong password"` — repeat per person, including one for yourself.
4. Tell each person their username/password directly (text, call, in person) — never put passwords in the repo or commit history.

You can also run steps 2–3 for free in a **GitHub Codespace** on this repo
instead of your own machine, if you'd rather not install Node locally.

### 5. Publish to GitHub Pages
1. Push this folder to a GitHub repo.
2. Repo → **Settings → Pages → Source** → deploy from the branch/folder containing `index.html` (root is fine).
3. Wait for the green checkmark, then open the `https://<you>.github.io/<repo>/` URL it gives you — that's the link you share with people (along with their personal username/password, separately).

## Managing accounts later
```
node create-user.js add <username> <password>     # add someone
node create-user.js remove <username>              # revoke access
node create-user.js list                            # see all usernames
```

## Limitations worth knowing
- **No self-signup, by design.** If someone forgets their password, only you can create a fresh one (there's no "forgot password" flow wired up, since Auth accounts use a fake `@wire.local` email rather than a real one you'd get reset emails through).
- **No message editing/deleting** in the current rules (`update, delete: if false`) — kept simple and tamper-evident. Relax those lines in `firestore.rules` if you want it.
- **No push notifications.** People need the tab open to see messages arrive live; reopening it re-shows full history. Adding email/Telegram pings for new messages is a reasonable next step if you want it, and can be done later without touching this architecture.
- **Everyone with an account can see the full username list** (needed so people can pick who to DM). Don't create accounts for people you don't want to be discoverable to each other.